div.p:
In the Hypotyposeis, to put it briefly, he has made abridged narratives of all the canonical Scripture, not passing over even the disputed books—I mean that of Jude and the rest of the catholic epistles, and that of Barnabas, and the so-called Apocalypse of Peter.
2. And the Epistle to the Hebrews he says is Paul's, but written to Hebrews in the Hebrew tongue, and that Luke, having carefully translated it, published it for the Greeks; whence the same complexion is found in respect of the rendering of this epistle and of the Acts. He says that the words 'Paul an apostle' were naturally not prefixed;
3. 'for,' he says, 'writing to the Hebrews, who had conceived a prejudice against him and were suspicious of him, very wisely he did not at the outset repel them by setting down his name.'
4. Then a little below he adds: 'But now, as the blessed presbyter used to say, since the Lord, being the apostle of the Almighty, was sent to the Hebrews, Paul, through modesty, as having been sent to the Gentiles, does not inscribe himself an apostle of the Hebrews, both on account of the honour due to the Lord, and because he wrote to the Hebrews also out of abundance, being a herald and apostle of the Gentiles.'
5. Again, in the same books, Clement has set down a tradition of the primitive presbyters concerning the order of the Gospels, having this form. He said that those Gospels were written first which contain the genealogies;
6. but that the Gospel according to Mark had this dispensation. When Peter had publicly preached the word at Rome and set forth the gospel by the Spirit, those present, being many, exhorted Mark, as one who had followed him from afar and remembered the things spoken, to write down what had been said; and having done so, he imparted the gospel to those who asked of him;
7. and when Peter learned of it, he neither hindered nor exhorted. But John, last of all, perceiving that the bodily things had been set forth in the Gospels, being urged by his acquaintances and divinely moved by the Spirit, composed a spiritual Gospel. So much Clement.
8. Again, the aforesaid Alexander makes mention of Clement, and also of Pantaenus, in a certain letter to Origen, as men known to him; he writes thus: 'For this also has been the will of God, as you know, that the friendship that has come down to us from our forefathers should remain inviolate—nay, rather more fervent and more firm.'
9. 'For we know as fathers those blessed ones who went before us, with whom we shall be a little hence: Pantaenus, the truly blessed man and lord, and the holy Clement, who was my lord and profited me, and any other such; through whom I came to know you, the man in all things most excellent, my lord and brother.' And these things are so.
10. Moreover, Adamantius—for this too was a name of Origen—when Zephyrinus in these times was governing the church of the Romans, sojourned at Rome, as he himself somewhere writes, saying: 'Having prayed to see the most ancient church of the Romans.' There, after staying no long time, he returned to Alexandria;
11. and indeed he fulfilled there the customary duties of catechizing with all zeal, Demetrius the bishop there still at that time urging him on and all but entreating him to bestow unwearyingly his benefit upon the brethren.

But to those things this should be added: that in the sixth book of his Expositions of the Gospel according to John he indicates that he had composed the first five while still at Alexandria; and of his work on the whole of this same gospel only twenty-two volumes have come down to us.
2. In the ninth of those On Genesis (and they are twelve in all) he discloses that he had written, before the ninth, not only commentaries at Alexandria, but also on the first twenty-five Psalms, and besides those on the Lamentations, of which there have come down to us five volumes, in which he mentions also the books On the Resurrection;
3. and these too are two. Moreover, he wrote the books On First Principles before his removal from Alexandria; and the work entitled Stromateis, ten in number, he composed in the same city, in the reign of Alexander, as the notes in his own hand prefixed to the volumes also show.

Gordian having succeeded Maximinus in the rule of the Romans, in the church at Rome Pontian, who had been bishop six years, was succeeded by Anteros, and he by Fabian, who had ministered a month;
2. they say that Fabian, after the death of Anteros, having come from the country together with others, was sojourning at Rome, where most marvellously, by the divine and heavenly grace, he came to the clergy.
3. For when all the brethren were assembled for the choosing of him who should succeed to the bishopric, and very many distinguished and renowned men were in the minds of the multitude, Fabian, though present, came into no man's thought; nevertheless, they record, all of a sudden a dove flying down from on high settled upon his head, showing forth a likeness of the descent of the Holy Spirit, in the form of a dove, upon the Saviour;
4. whereupon all the people, as if moved by one divine Spirit, with all eagerness and one soul cried out that he was worthy, and without delay took him and placed him upon the throne of the bishopric. Then too, the bishop at Antioch, Zebennus, having departed this life, Babylas succeeded to the office; and in Alexandria, Heraclas having received the ministry after Demetrius, Dionysius succeeded to the charge of the catechizing there, being himself also one of the pupils of Origen.

Those of Gallus's party, having held the power not even two full years, were removed out of the way, and Valerian together with his son Gallienus succeeded to the rule.
2. Again, what Dionysius relates concerning this man may be learned from his letter to Hermammon, in which he records thus: 'And to John likewise it is revealed: "And there was given to him," he says, "a mouth speaking great things and blasphemy, and there was given to him authority and forty-two months."
3. Both things are wonderful in Valerian, and most of all the first, how he behaved—to consider how gentle and kindly he was toward the men of God; for none of the emperors before him was so favourably and graciously disposed toward them, not even those said to have been openly Christians, as he plainly was when at the first he received them most intimately and affectionately; and his whole house was filled with godly persons and was a church of God;
4. but the teacher and ruler of the synagogue of the magi from Egypt persuaded him to put them away, bidding him slay and persecute the pure and holy men as opponents and hinderers of his all-abominable and detestable enchantments (for they are, and were, able, when present and seen and merely breathing and speaking, to scatter the plots of the noxious demons), and suggesting that he perform unhallowed rites and accursed sorceries and ill-omened sacrifices, slaughtering wretched boys and sacrificing the children of unhappy fathers, and dividing the entrails of the newborn, and cutting up and mangling the formations of God, as though they would thereby attain happiness.'
5. And to these he adds, saying: 'Fine thank-offerings, then, did Macrianus bring them for the empire he hoped for; who, formerly being called the emperor's accountant-general, thought nothing reasonable nor universal, but has fallen under the prophetic curse that says: "Woe to those who prophesy out of their own heart and see not the universal";
6. for he did not understand the universal providence, nor regard the judgment of Him who is before all and through all and over all; whence he became an enemy of His catholic Church, and alienated and estranged himself from the mercy of God, and banished himself as far as possible from his own salvation, in this making good his own name.'
7. And again, after other matters, he says: 'For Valerian, led by this man to these things, given over to insults and reproaches, according to what was said to Isaiah: "And these have chosen their own ways and their abominations, which their soul desired; and I will choose their mockeries, and will requite them their sins";
8. but this man, madly desiring the empire beyond his desert, and unable, with his maimed body, to put on the imperial array, set forward his two sons, who took upon themselves their father's sins. For clear in their case is the prediction which God spoke: "Requiting the sins of fathers upon children unto the third and fourth generation, to those who hate me."
9. For having cast his own wicked desires, in which he failed, upon the heads of his sons, he wiped off upon them his own wickedness and his hatred toward God.' So much Dionysius concerning Valerian.

But all the indignities and pains which the martyrs in the Thebaid endured surpass all account—scraped over the whole body with potsherds instead of claws, even until they departed life; and women, bound by one foot and raised aloft into the air, head downward by certain machines, their bodies wholly naked and uncovered, presenting this most shameful and of all the cruellest and most inhuman spectacle to all who beheld;
2. while others again died bound to trees and stumps; for, drawing together by certain machines the strongest of the branches, and stretching to each of these one of the martyr's legs, they let the branches go to be carried back to their own nature, devising a sudden tearing apart of the limbs against those upon whom they attempted these things.
3. And all these things were wrought not for a few days or for some brief time, but over a long interval of whole years, sometimes more than ten, sometimes above twenty in number being put to death, at other times no fewer than thirty, sometimes near sixty, and again at another time a hundred men in a single day, together with quite young children and women, were slain, condemned to manifold and changing punishments.
4. We ourselves too, being on the spot, observed many at once in a single day, some undergoing beheading, some the punishment by fire, so that the murdering iron was blunted and, growing weak, was broken in pieces, and the executioners themselves, growing weary, relieved one another in turns;
5. when also we beheld the most wonderful impulse and the truly divine power and eagerness of those who had believed in the Christ of God. For as soon as sentence was pronounced against the former, others from this side and that leapt up to the tribunal before the judge, confessing themselves Christians, taking no thought for the terrors and the manifold kinds of tortures, but undismayed speaking freely of their piety toward the God of all, and with joy and laughter and gladness receiving the final sentence of death, so that they sang psalms and sent up hymns and thanksgivings to the God of all up to their very last breath.
6. Wonderful, then, were these too; but exceptionally more wonderful were those who, distinguished by wealth and noble birth and glory, by learning and philosophy, yet set all these in the second place to true religion and to faith in our Saviour and Lord Jesus Christ:
7. such as Philoromus was, who had been entrusted with no ordinary office of the imperial administration at Alexandria, and who, with his dignity and Roman honour, escorted by soldiers, was examined day by day; and Phileas, bishop of the church of the Thmuites, a man distinguished both in the public life and offices of his country and in philosophic discourse;
8. who, though countless of their kinsmen and other friends entreated them, and besides the rulers in office, and the judge himself exhorting that they should take pity on themselves and spare their children and wives, were by no means led by so many to choose to love their life and to despise the laws of our Saviour concerning confession and denial; but with manly and philosophic reasoning, or rather with a pious and God-loving soul, standing firm against all the judge's threats and insults, both were beheaded.

And so, wrestling with so many evils, he came to a consciousness of the things he had dared against the godly; and gathering his mind to himself, he first made acknowledgment to the God of all, and then, calling those about him, commanded them without any delay to put a stop to the persecution against the Christians, and by law and imperial decree to hasten the building of their churches and the performance of their customary rites, while they offered prayers for the imperial house.
2. At once, then, deed following upon word, imperial edicts were published throughout the cities, containing the recantation of the things against us in this manner:
3. 'The Emperor Caesar Galerius Valerius Maximianus, Invincible, Augustus, Pontifex Maximus, Germanicus Maximus, Aegyptiacus Maximus, Thebaicus Maximus, Sarmaticus Maximus five times, Persicus Maximus twice, Carpicus Maximus six times, Armeniacus Maximus, Medicus Maximus, Adiabenicus Maximus, holding the tribunician power for the twentieth time, Imperator for the nineteenth, Consul for the eighth, Father of his Country, Proconsul;
4. and the Emperor Caesar Flavius Valerius Constantinus, Pious, Fortunate, Invincible, Augustus, Pontifex Maximus, holding the tribunician power, Imperator for the fifth time, Consul, Father of his Country, Proconsul;
5. and the Emperor Caesar Valerius Licinianus Licinius, Pious, Fortunate, Invincible, Augustus, Pontifex Maximus, holding the tribunician power for the fourth time, Imperator for the third, Consul, Father of his Country, Proconsul, to their own provincials, greeting.
6. Among the rest of the things which we ordain for the advantage and benefit of the state, we for our part formerly wished to set all things right according to the ancient laws and public order of the Romans, and to make this provision: that the Christians too, who had forsaken the way of life of their own parents, should return to a good purpose;
7. since by some reasoning such wilfulness had seized them and such folly had overtaken them, that they did not follow the things established by the ancients—which perhaps their own parents had formerly instituted—but according to their own purpose and as each one wished, so made laws for themselves and observed these, and gathered in various places various multitudes.
8. Accordingly, when such a command had followed from us, that they should betake themselves to the things established by the ancients, very many were subjected to peril, and very many were harassed and underwent all manner of deaths;
9. and since most of them persisted in the same folly, and we saw that they neither rendered to the heavenly gods the worship due, nor attended to that of the Christians, looking to our humanity and the perpetual custom by which we are accustomed to grant pardon to all men, we have thought it right to extend our indulgence most readily in this matter also, so that they may again be Christians and may rebuild the houses in which they used to assemble, in such a way that they do nothing contrary to public order. And by another letter we shall make known to the judges what they will have to observe;
10. wherefore, in accordance with this our indulgence, they ought to entreat their own God for our welfare and that of the state and their own, that the state may in every way be kept sound, and that they may be able to live free from care in their own homes.'
11. This, in the Roman tongue, rendered as far as possible into the Greek language, was of this form. It is now time to consider what came to pass after these things.

This man having first acted thus by his own resolve, all the rest of those in office who dwelt in the cities under the same rule were moved to make the like decree, the governors of the provinces having perceived that this was pleasing to the emperor, and having suggested to their subjects to do this very thing;
2. and when the tyrant had most gladly assented by rescript to their decrees, the persecution against us was again kindled afresh from the beginning. Then indeed priests of the idols were appointed in each city, and over these high priests, by Maximinus himself, those who had most distinguished themselves in public life and become renowned in every way; and great was the zeal introduced among them concerning the worship of those they served.
3. Indeed, the extraordinary superstition of the ruler, to speak briefly, led all under him, both rulers and ruled, to do everything against us for his favour, requiting him with this as the greatest favour, in return for the benefits they thought to obtain from him—to thirst for our blood and to display against us certain newer cruelties.
